{
  "summary": "The Clean Architecture audit reveals a health score of 60 out of 100, driven by six critical layer-boundary violations that invert the intended dependency direction. These violations span from Application layer modules improperly reaching into Presentation and Infrastructure, to Domain logic directly coupling with infrastructure and presentation concerns. No circular dependencies were detected, but all violations stem from the same root issue—a failure to respect the inward-pointing dependency rule central to Clean Architecture."
,
  "rationales": [
    {
      "violation_id": "main->presentation.api",
      "why_it_matters": "Application-layer 'main' importing 'presentation.api' forces the inner business layer to depend on outer delivery mechanics, creating tight coupling and undermining modularity.",
      "prescribed_fix": "Define a controller or entry-point port in the Application layer, with 'presentation.api' injecting the Application service via dependency injection rather than being imported as a dependency."
    },
    {
      "violation_id": "main->infrastructure.database",
      "why_it_matters": "Importing Infrastructure from Application breaks the layering principle and hardcodes persistence concerns into the core orchestration logic.",
      "prescribed_fix": "Create an abstract DatabaseService interface in Application, implemented as an adapter in Infrastructure, and inject the interface at runtime using a composition root."
    },
    {
      "violation_id": "domain.fraud_checker->infrastructure.database",
      "why_it_matters": "Domain should be a pure, framework-agnostic core. Directly importing Infrastructure pollutes the domain with I/O concerns and makes unit testing impossible without real database connections.",
      "prescribed_fix": "Introduce a Repository port (interface) in the Application or Domain layer, then implement a concrete adapter in Infrastructure that satisfies this port, and inject it where the domain uses data access."
    },
    {
      "violation_id": "domain.payment_rule->presentation.controllers",
      "why_it_matters": "Domain use cases importing Presentation components violates the fundamental clean architecture rule: dependencies must point inward. It tightly couples business logic to output formatting.",
      "prescribed_fix": "Extract data from 'payment_rule' as plain models, pass them to Presentation via interfaces or DTOs, and let Presentation handle formatting without the Domain knowing about it."
    },
    {
      "violation_id": "infrastructure.audit_logger->presentation.auth_middleware",
      "why_it_matters": "Infrastructure adapters should be agnostic of web frameworks. Importing auth middleware couples the persistence logging layer to HTTP session management, reducing testability and portability.",
      "prescribed_fix": "Pass authentication context as parameters from Application to Infrastructure, or define an AuthContext interface in Application and inject a Presentation-backed implementation."
    },
    {
      "violation_id": "presentation.order_routes->infrastructure.database",
      "why_it_matters": "Presentation directly accessing Infrastructure bypasses use cases and business logic, making routes untestable in isolation and hard to maintain when persistence evolves.",
      "prescribed_fix": "Route incoming requests to an Application service or use-case that orchestrates the operation; Presentation only serializes responses, never touches data sources directly."
    }
  ],
  "roadmap": [
    "address all 6 violations (all major severity) by introducing proper ports and adapters to reverse the current dependency flow.",
    "establish clear layering rules enforced by architecture linting: Domain < Application < Presentation, with Infrastructure only depending on outer layers through abstractions.",
    "create an architectural compliance test suite to detect future layer violations in CI/CD pipelines."
  ]
}

